<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE CAPABILITY FIXTURE. R-1's reason for existing.

  One internal general entity, declared in the internal subset, referenced in content.
  No file, no URL, no recursion — nothing a <!DOCTYPE pre-reject, a network block or a
  size cap could stop. The ONLY thing that decides the outcome is whether the parser
  parses the internal subset:

      an EXPANDING parser  -> <title> reads "AAA"
      parse-xml            -> throws "Named entity isn't defined: &a;"

  So this document is the one whose result DIFFERS between the two, which is what makes
  an assertion on it falsifiable. The classic XXE fixtures do not have that property
  behind the port's DOCTYPE gate — SR-H-1, the finding this whole criterion comes from:
  the ADR's XXE assertion passed even for an expanding parser, because the gate answered
  first. `parse-xml.adapter.capability.spec.ts` therefore runs THIS file against the
  adapter directly, with the gate bypassed, and asserts both that it raises and that no
  result anywhere contains "AAA".
-->
<!DOCTYPE r [
  <!ENTITY a "AAA">
]>
<r><title>&a;</title></r>
